       pcap-filter - packet filter syntax

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       ppccaapp__ccoommppiillee(3) is used to compile a string into a filter program.  The
       resulting filter program can then be applied to some stream of  packets
       to  determine which packets will be supplied to ppccaapp__lloooopp(3), ppccaapp__ddiiss‐‐
       ppaattcchh(3), ppccaapp__nneexxtt(3), or ppccaapp__nneexxtt__eexx(3).

       The _f_i_l_t_e_r _e_x_p_r_e_s_s_i_o_n consists of one or more  _p_r_i_m_i_t_i_v_e_s.   Primitives
       usually consist of an _i_d (name or number) preceded by one or more qual‐
       ifiers.  There are three different kinds of qualifier:

       _t_y_p_e   _t_y_p_e qualifiers say what kind of thing the  id  name  or  number
              refers  to.   Possible  types are hhoosstt, nneett, ppoorrtt and ppoorrttrraannggee.
              E.g., `hhoosstt foo', `nneett 128.3', `ppoorrtt 20', `ppoorrttrraannggee 6000-6008'.
              If there is no type qualifier, hhoosstt is assumed.

       _d_i_r    _d_i_r qualifiers specify a particular transfer direction to and/or
              from _i_d.  Possible directions are ssrrcc, ddsstt, ssrrcc oorr ddsstt, ssrrcc  aanndd
              ddsstt,  rraa,  ttaa, aaddddrr11, aaddddrr22, aaddddrr33, and aaddddrr44.  E.g., `ssrrcc foo',
              `ddsstt nneett 128.3', `ssrrcc oorr ddsstt ppoorrtt ftp-data'.  If there is no dir
              qualifier,  `ssrrcc  oorr ddsstt' is assumed.  The rraa, ttaa, aaddddrr11, aaddddrr22,
              aaddddrr33, and aaddddrr44 qualifiers are only valid for IEEE 802.11 Wire‐
              less LAN link layers.

       _p_r_o_t_o  _p_r_o_t_o  qualifiers  restrict  the match to a particular protocol.
              Possible protocols are: eetthheerr, ffddddii, ttrr,  wwllaann,  iipp,  iipp66,  aarrpp,
              rraarrpp,  ddeeccnneett,  ssccttpp,  ttccpp and uuddpp.  E.g., `eetthheerr ssrrcc foo', `aarrpp
              nneett 128.3', `ttccpp ppoorrtt  21',  `uuddpp  ppoorrttrraannggee  7000-7009',  `wwllaann
              aaddddrr22  0:2:3:4:5:6'.  If there is no _p_r_o_t_o qualifier, all proto‐
              cols consistent with the type  are  assumed.   E.g.,  `ssrrcc  foo'
              means `((iipp66 oorr iipp oorr aarrpp oorr rraarrpp)) ssrrcc foo', `nneett bar' means `((iipp
              oorr aarrpp oorr rraarrpp)) nneett bar' and `ppoorrtt 53' means  `((ttccpp  oorr  uuddpp  oorr
              ssccttpp))  ppoorrtt  53' (note that these examples use invalid syntax to
              illustrate the principle).

       [ffddddii is actually an alias for eetthheerr; the parser  treats  them  identi‐
       cally  as  meaning  ``the data link level used on the specified network
       interface''.  FDDI headers contain Ethernet-like source and destination
       addresses,  and  often  contain  Ethernet-like packet types, so you can
       filter on these FDDI fields just as with the analogous Ethernet fields.
       FDDI  headers  also  contain other fields, but you cannot name them ex‐
       plicitly in a filter expression.

       Similarly, ttrr and wwllaann are aliases for eetthheerr; the previous  paragraph's
       statements about FDDI headers also apply to Token Ring and 802.11 wire‐
       less LAN headers.  For 802.11 headers, the destination address  is  the
       DA  field and the source address is the SA field; the BSSID, RA, and TA
       fields aren't tested.]

       In addition to the above, there are some special  `primitive'  keywords
       that  don't  follow  the pattern: ggaatteewwaayy, bbrrooaaddccaasstt, lleessss, ggrreeaatteerr and
       arithmetic expressions.  All of these are described below.

       More complex filter expressions are built up by using the words aanndd, oorr
       and  nnoott  (or equivalently: `&&&&', `||||' and `!!' respectively) to combine
       primitives.  E.g., `hhoosstt foo aanndd nnoott ppoorrtt ftp aanndd nnoott  ppoorrtt  ftp-data'.
       To  save  typing, identical qualifier lists can be omitted.  E.g., `ttccpp
       ddsstt ppoorrtt ftp oorr ftp-data oorr domain' is exactly the  same  as  `ttccpp  ddsstt
       ppoorrtt ftp oorr ttccpp ddsstt ppoorrtt ftp-data oorr ttccpp ddsstt ppoorrtt domain'.

       Allowable primitives are:

       ddsstt hhoosstt _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True  if  the  IPv4/v6  destination field of the packet is _h_o_s_t_‐
              _n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r, which may be either an address or a name.

       ssrrcc hhoosstt _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if the IPv4/v6 source field of the packet is _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.

       hhoosstt _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if either the IPv4/v6 source or destination of  the  packet
              is _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.

              Any of the above host expressions can be prepended with the key‐
              words, iipp, aarrpp, rraarrpp, or iipp66 as in:
                   iipp hhoosstt _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              which is equivalent to:
                   eetthheerr pprroottoo \\ip aanndd hhoosstt _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              If _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r is a name with multiple IPv4/v6 addresses,  each
              address will be checked for a match.

       eetthheerr ddsstt _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if the Ethernet destination address is _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.  _e_t_h_‐
              _e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r may be either a name from /etc/ethers or a  numerical
              MAC     address     of     the     form     "xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx",
              "xx.xx.xx.xx.xx.xx",   "xx-xx-xx-xx-xx-xx",    "xxxx.xxxx.xxxx",
              "xxxxxxxxxxxx",  or  various  mixes  of ':', '.', and '-', where
              each "x" is a hex digit (0-9, a-f, or A-F).

       eetthheerr ssrrcc _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if the Ethernet source address is _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.

       eetthheerr hhoosstt _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if either the Ethernet source  or  destination  address  is
              _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.

       ggaatteewwaayy _h_o_s_t
              True  if  the packet used _h_o_s_t as a gateway.  I.e., the Ethernet
              source or destination address was _h_o_s_t but neither the IP source
              nor  the  IP destination was _h_o_s_t.  _H_o_s_t must be a name and must
              be found both by the machine's  host-name-to-IP-address  resolu‐
              tion  mechanisms (host name file, DNS, NIS, etc.) and by the ma‐
              chine's   host-name-to-Ethernet-address   resolution   mechanism
              (/etc/ethers, etc.).  (An equivalent expression is
                   eetthheerr hhoosstt _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r aanndd nnoott hhoosstt _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              which  can be used with either names or numbers for _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              _/ _e_t_h_e_r_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.)  This syntax does  not  work  in  IPv6-enabled
              configuration at this moment.

       ddsstt nneett _n_e_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if the IPv4/v6 destination address of the packet has a net‐
              work number of _n_e_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.  _N_e_t may be either a name  from  the
              networks database (/etc/networks, etc.) or a network number.  An
              IPv4 network number can be  written  as  a  dotted  quad  (e.g.,
              192.168.1.0), dotted triple (e.g., 192.168.1), dotted pair (e.g,
              172.16),  or  single  number  (e.g.,   10);   the   netmask   is
              255.255.255.255  for a dotted quad (which means that it's really
              a host match), 255.255.255.0 for a  dotted  triple,  255.255.0.0
              for  a  dotted  pair, or 255.0.0.0 for a single number.  An IPv6
              network number  must  be  written  out  fully;  the  netmask  is
              ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff,   so   IPv6   "network"
              matches are really always host matches, and a network match  re‐
              quires a netmask length.

       ssrrcc nneett _n_e_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True  if  the IPv4/v6 source address of the packet has a network
              number of _n_e_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.

       nneett _n_e_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r
              True if either the IPv4/v6 source or destination address of  the
              packet has a network number of _n_e_t_n_a_m_e_a_d_d_r.

       nneett _n_e_t_a_d_d_r mmaasskk _n_e_t_m_a_s_k
              True  if the IPv4 address matches _n_e_t_a_d_d_r with the specific _n_e_t_‐
              _m_a_s_k.  May be qualified with ssrrcc or ddsstt.  Note that this  syntax
              is not valid for IPv6 _n_e_t_a_d_d_r.

       nneett _n_e_t_a_d_d_r/_l_e_n
              True  if  the IPv4/v6 address matches _n_e_t_a_d_d_r with a netmask _l_e_n
              bits wide.  May be qualified with ssrrcc or ddsstt.

       ddsstt ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m
              True if the packet is IPv4/v6 TCP, UDP or SCTP and has a  desti‐
              nation port value of _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m.  The _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m can be a num‐
              ber or a name used in /etc/services (see ttccpp(4P)  and  uuddpp(4P)).
              If  a  name  is  used,  both  the  port  number and protocol are
              checked.  If a number or ambiguous name is used, only  the  port
              number  is checked (e.g., `ddsstt ppoorrtt 513' will print both tcp/lo‐
              gin traffic and udp/who traffic, and `ppoorrtt  domain'  will  print
              both tcp/domain and udp/domain traffic).

       ssrrcc ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m
              True if the packet has a source port value of _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m.

       ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m
              True  if  either the source or destination port of the packet is
              _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m.

       ddsstt ppoorrttrraannggee _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1_-_p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2
              True if the packet is IPv4/v6 TCP, UDP or SCTP and has a  desti‐
              nation  port  value  between _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1 and _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2 (both
              inclusive).  _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1 and _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2  are  interpreted  in
              the same fashion as the _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m parameter for ppoorrtt.

       ssrrcc ppoorrttrraannggee _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1_-_p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2
              True  if the packet has a source port value between _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1
              and _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2 (both inclusive).

       ppoorrttrraannggee _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1_-_p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2
              True if either the source or destination port of the  packet  is
              between _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_1 and _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m_2 (both inclusive).

              Any of the above port or port range expressions can be prepended
              with the keywords, ttccpp, uuddpp or ssccttpp, as in:
                   ttccpp ssrrcc ppoorrtt _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m
              which matches only TCP packets whose source port is _p_o_r_t_n_a_m_e_n_u_m.

       lleessss _l_e_n_g_t_h
              True if the packet has a length less than or  equal  to  _l_e_n_g_t_h.
              This is equivalent to:
                   lleenn <<== _l_e_n_g_t_h

       ggrreeaatteerr _l_e_n_g_t_h
